    "chunk_text": "for the countries bordering the Indian Ocean, it is also essential for the stability of the global economy. Sri Lanka, therefore, calls upon all parties involved in regional tensions to exercise the utmost restraint and pursue dialogue and diplomatic engagement as the primary means of resolving disputes. Escalation of tension benefits no one; peace benefits everyone.\n\nMadam, in recent months, our nation itself experienced how quickly unexpected events could test our resilience. The devastation caused by Cyclone \"Ditwah\" affected communities across the parts of the country. Homes were damaged; livelihoods were disrupted and many families faced difficult circumstances. Yet, during that period, we also witnessed the remarkable strength and solidarity of the Sri Lankan people; citizens supported one another. Public institutions worked tirelessly to resolve services. Our armed forces and civil authorities stood beside the people during their most difficult tasks. And also, the international community came forward to help the Sri Lankan people. That spirit of unity remains one of greatest strengths of our nation. Madam, Sri Lanka may be a small nation in the vast landscape of geopolitics. But, the greatness of a nation is not measured by its size; it is measured by humanity. We may be a small nation, but we are a generous nation. When lives are in danger, we respond with compassion. When people seek assistance, we extend our hand. Our people understand that humanity must always prevail over division.\n\nMadam, in a world where tensions, sometimes, overshadow cooperation, we must continue to hold on to the value that unite humanity. As the words \u201cYou may say I am a dreamer, but I am not the only one\u201d of a famous song once reminded the world, the dream of peace is not the dream of one nation alone, but it is a shared aspiration of humanity. Sri Lanka will continue to stand on the side of that dream, a dream of a peaceful"
